  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nSurface anticyclone will keep conditions mild for the next few days \nacross the lower Missouri River Valley, while the mid and upper-\nlevel pattern will have a weak northwesterly component to it. Clear \nskies and efficient radiational cooling may allow lows to dip into \nthe lower 30s for areas south of Interstate 70 late tonight and \nearly Sunday, leading to the potential for Frost Conditions. \nTherefore, have issued a Frost Advisory for this part of the \nforecast area for Sunday morning. A closed 500mb low moves into the \nsouthern Plains Sunday and Monday, while a 500mb ridge attempts to \npush northeastward into the Central and Eastern Plains. 850mb flow \nturns south-southwesterly with this feature, and will provide WAA \ninto the region, which will push temperatures back into the 60s to \nstart the next work week.  \n\nBy the middle of next week, several mid-level features develop. H5 \nridge axis is progged to amplify across the western CONUS, while a \nshort-trough with strong vort maxima moves downstream across the \nIntermountain West and heads toward the Central and Southern Plains. \nAnother mid-level trough with strong vort maxima moves across the \nCanadian Prairie Provinces and heads for the Great Lakes Region. \nWhat remains uncertain at this point, is if these two trough axes \nwill phase or not. Current guidance suggests that these two waves do \nnot phase until after they are further east of our forecast area. So \nwhile a surface cyclone is expected to develop with stronger dCVA, \na lot of the forcing is either too far south or too far northeast of \nthe forecast, which would keep conditions mostly dry except for our \nsoutheastern zones. If the wave over the Intermountain West slows \ndown, we may see more middle and upper-level support that would \nbring rain showers to central and eastern Missouri. For right now, \nslight chance POPs will be focused primarily south of Interstate 70 \nthrough the middle of this week. Toward the end of the work week, \nnorthwesterly flow will bring CAA, sending temperatures back in the \nmid 50s for highs.",
